## Exercise 2: The Ration Calculator

Work out how many crates of food a voyage needs.

Two constants: three meals per day, and 24 rations per crate. Ask for the crew size and the number of days at sea, then work out the meals needed and the crates to load. A part-full crate still has to be loaded, so round up. Finish by saying either how many rations the last crate has spare, or that every crate is full.

A sample run, where the person typed **5** and then **10**:

```
Crew size:
Days at sea:
Meals needed: 150
Rations per crate: 24
Crates to load: 7
The last crate will have 18 rations spare
```

Rounding up is the whole exercise, and it is the seating plan from Lesson 3.7 again: `//` gives the full crates, `%` gives the remainder, and a remainder above zero means one more crate. Do not reach for `/` and `round()` — that rounds to nearest, which loads six crates for 150 meals and leaves six people hungry.

The spare capacity is `RATIONS_PER_CRATE - spare`, which is worth thinking about for a moment rather than copying: if six rations spill into the last crate, that crate has eighteen empty slots.

The second test case comes out exactly even, and takes the other branch.

