## What You Learned in Chapter 3

Look at what Exercise 3 needed. Every one of these was a lesson:

- **Variables** — a label on a value, reassigned as the program runs, and four of them updated together (3.1)
- **Integers and floats** — counting in whole numbers, dividing once at the end, and rounding for display (3.2)
- **Strings** — `len()`, indexing from nought, and building text up one character at a time (3.3)
- **Booleans and decisions** — comparisons producing values, and `if`/`elif`/`else` acting on them (3.4)
- **Loops that ask** — `while True:` with a sentinel and `break`, running an unknown number of times (3.5)
- **Type conversion** — `int()` on what a person typed, and the `ValueError` waiting if you convert too soon (3.6)
- **Constants and style** — the good-day threshold named once at the top, where it can be found and changed (3.7)

## What Comes Next

**Chapter 4: Functions & Parameters.**

You have written functions before — Karel was full of them. But a Karel function could only ever *do* something: it took nothing in, gave nothing back, and communicated purely by changing the world around it. Every program in this chapter has been one long block of code for exactly that reason.

Chapter 4 opens the data path in both directions. Functions take **parameters**, so the same function can work on different values, and they **return** results, so a function can answer a question rather than just perform an action. That is what turns decomposition from a tidying exercise into the main tool you have for building anything large.

You will also find out that the functions you have been calling all along — `print()`, `input()`, `len()`, `int()`, `range()` — were never special. They are ordinary functions, and by the end of that chapter you will be able to write your own versions of most of them.

